{
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "title",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Module 15 — Network Logs Monitoring and Analysis\nWhat a log is → Windows → Linux → Mac → firewalls → routers → web servers → the 7-step centralized pipeline",
      "x": -1200,
      "y": -3600,
      "width": 2400,
      "height": 70,
      "color": "5"
    },
    {
      "id": "b1",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "LO01 · WHY LOG  (pp4-13)\n\n4 TYPES OF LOGGING\n  Security — identifying and RESPONDING to\n    threats, viruses, malware, data loss\n  Operational — system-processing activities,\n    failures, service provisioning\n  Compliance — \"a PART OF SECURITY LOGGING\"\n  Application debug — for DEVELOPERS,\n    not system administrators\n\n2 TRANSFER MECHANISMS\n  PUSH — save to local disk or send over the\n    network; needs a LOG COLLECTOR\n    syslog + SNMP = the two main push protocols\n  PULL — client-server; PROPRIETARY format\n    eg Check Point OPSEC C library\n\n6 NEEDS · identify security incidents · monitor\n  policy violations · identify fraudulent activity\n  · identify operational and long-term problems\n  · establish baselines · ensure compliance\n\nTRAP: p8 — ordinary logs are NOT enough to\n  troubleshoot NETWORK problems. \"Syslog need\n  to be utilized for this purpose\"",
      "x": -2400,
      "y": -3420,
      "width": 560,
      "height": 700
    },
    {
      "id": "b1b",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "LO01 · THE REQUIREMENTS  (pp9-11)\n\nBEFORE ENABLING LOGGING, YOU SHOULD KNOW\n  what to log · where to store the logs\n  · methods for logging · tools required\n  · log format\n\n9 REQUIREMENTS FOR LOGGING  ← the yield\n  determine applications and systems (incl.\n    outsourced / cloud) with event logging\n  configure the IS for correct security incidents\n  regular tuning and review to minimize FALSE\n    POSITIVES\n  store events in event logs\n  NORMALIZE and AGGREGATE security events\n  correlate the data sources for malicious activity\n  SYNCHRONIZE TIMESTAMPS of all sources\n    (→ without this, correlation fails)\n  prevent unauthorized access/manipulation of logs\n  analyze security-based events in the event logs\n\n  ✗ the 5-item \"You should be able to\" callout on\n    the SAME page is a subset; it prints\n    \"manipulation TO the logs\" vs \"OF the logs\"\n\n10 THINGS A LOG CARRIES\n  user identification information · date and time\n  · type of event · success or failure indication\n  · event origination point · description\n  · severity · service name · protocol · user\n\nFORENSICS: logs are a \"permanent source of record\n  that cannot be altered\" — chronological, and a\n  BACKUP source of evidence if the original was\n  tampered with",
      "x": -1740,
      "y": -3420,
      "width": 600,
      "height": 700
    },
    {
      "id": "b2",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "LO02 · WINDOWS  (pp14-35)\n\nEvent Viewer; log source in the registry\n\nTHE .EVT FILE\n  ELF LOGFILE HEADER + EVENTLOGRECORD blocks\n  HeaderSize  → 0 x 30\n  Signature    → 0x654c664c  AND  Ox654c664c\n                (the page prints \"Ox\" for hex 0)\n  p18 figure: records WRAP — nos 102, 103,\n    299, 300, 301, 400\n\n✗ Table 15.1/15.3 print 20 member types against\n  only 16 named members. Both reproduced.\n\nTHE 10 FIELDS OF A LOG ENTRY  (pp24-25)\n  Level   → Error, Warning, Information,\n            Success Audit, Failure Audit\n  Keywords → AuditFailure, AuditSuccess, Classic,\n            Correlation Hint, Response Time, SQM,\n            WDI Context, WDI Diag\n  Date and time · Source\n  Event ID → \"a UNIQUE event ID is assigned for\n            each type of event\"\n  Task category (page prints \"category of ask\")\n  User · Operational code · Log · Computer\n\n  ✗ p23 prints a 6-item version of this list",
      "x": -1080,
      "y": -3420,
      "width": 600,
      "height": 700
    },
    {
      "id": "b3",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "LO03 · LINUX  (pp36-46)\n\n/var/log INVENTORY  (pp39-40)\n  messages · auth.log · kern.log · cron.log\n  maillog · mail.log · boot.log · daemon.log\n  mysqld.log\n  btmp    → \"a record of all UNSUCCESSFUL logon\n             attempts\"\n  xferlog → \"a record of FTP file transfers\"\n  Apache → access_log, error_log\n\n  ✗ paths print with stray spaces (\"auth. log\")\n    and the messages alias as \"/ vat/ log/ syslog\"\n    → reproduced as printed, NOT repaired\n\nsyslog.conf  →  SELECTOR.ACTION\n  *.info.none;news.none;authpriv.none;cron.none\n  kern.* · authpriv.* · mail.*\n  facilities: auth, authpriv, cron, kern, mail\n\nSEVERITY  (p43)  ← THE TRAP\n  printed Level column captures only SEVEN labels\n  printed Value column carries only 1-7\n  ✗ NO VALUE PRINTED FOR THE EMERGENCY ROW\n  prose: \"starting from level 0 to level 7 …\n    the highest severe message is at level 0\"\n  table prints \"Waming\"; prose \"Level 4—Warning\"\n  ✗ Alert extension prints \"-alert\" (hyphen)",
      "x": -420,
      "y": -3420,
      "width": 600,
      "height": 700,
      "color": "3"
    },
    {
      "id": "b4",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "LO04 · MAC  (pp47-58)\n\n5 TYPES OF LOG  (pp50-53)\n  Security  → secure.log, /private/var/log\n              login/logout; attempted and\n              successful unauthorized activity\n  Firewall  → appfirewall.log  (appfwlogd)\n  User-specific → \"found in THE FOLDER\"\n              ✗ THE PAGE NEVER NAMES IT\n  Command line → .bash_history, root's home\n  System    → system.log\n\n  ✗ p52 prints TEN locations against NINE names\n    (extra generic /Library/Logs row shifts it)\n\nLOG LINE FORMAT  (p54)  — only THREE parts\n  1 the date and time in the DD HH SS format\n  2 the host service\n  3 the messages\n  ✗ NEVER split into hostname/process; no\n    facility or severity field is named →\n    the field list was NOT expanded\n  ✗ the two printed Syntax: strings DISAGREE\n    (MW DD … vs ION DD HH:bN: ss …); both quoted,\n    NEITHER repaired into MMM DD HH:MM:SS\n\nthen system.log + the CONSOLE walkthrough\n  (the Finder / Go-to-folder / Find dialogs are\n   GUI → NOT read)",
      "x": 240,
      "y": -3420,
      "width": 600,
      "height": 700
    },
    {
      "id": "b5",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "LO05 · FIREWALLS, the shape  (pp59-62)\n\n\"The capability of a firewall to log users'\nactivities in a network\" — the page calls it\n\"the most important source for determining POST\nATTACK SCENARIOS\": ATTACKERS LEAVE FOOTPRINTS\n\nLEVELS O to 7 — level O greatest, 7 least\n  emergency, alert, critical, error, warning,\n  notification, informational, debugging\n  (printed as an ordered list, NO number beside\n   any name; level prints as the letter O)\n\n7 ITEMS TO LOOK FOR\n  rejected and dropped IP addresses\n  unsuccessful logins to firewall + critical servers\n  suspicious OUTBOUND from internal servers\n  SOURCE-ROUTED packets\n  ports on which no application is running\n  stop/start/restart of firewall\n  change in firewall configuration\n\n5 STEPS OF ANALYSIS\n  find the log file's location → identify and\n  analyze the fields → interpret incoming and\n  outgoing connections from different sources →\n  find source IP, destination IP and the action\n  taken → locate the source IP with IP address\n  tracking tools\n\n✗ p62 prints a lone bullet \"Tear down in\n  connection\" that pp59-62 never define",
      "x": 900,
      "y": -3420,
      "width": 600,
      "height": 700
    },
    {
      "id": "b6",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "LO05 · ONE VENDOR EACH  (pp63-88)\n\nWINDOWS DEFENDER FIREWALL  (pp63-68)\n  wf.msc · pfirewall.log\n  ✗ p63 callout \"C:\\Firewall\"  vs  p65 prose\n    \"\\LogFi1es\\Firewa11\\Pfirewa11. log\" —\n    the page contradicts itself\n  header (log version + available fields)\n    + body\n  \"If there is no value for a field, it is\n   represented by (-)\"\n  ✗ p63 vs p65 filename case also differs\n\nmacOS APPLICATION FIREWALL  (pp69-72)\n  ✗ p69 \"/private/var/log/\" vs p71 \"/var/log\"\n  Table 15.7 — 16 field names, 15 descriptions\n  ✗ AND its descriptions CONTRADICT its field\n    names: HOSTNAME = \"Client IP address\",\n    SERVER = \"Port to which access is attempted\"\n\nIPTABLES  (pp73-76)\n  /var/log/kern.log · tail -5 …/messages\n  ✗ the two printings of the sample line\n    disagree (IN=eth1 vs IN—ethi; Tos=oxoo vs\n    TOS=OxOO; WINDOW-32767 vs WINDOW=32767)\n  ✗ NO \"=\" PRINTS AFTER \"OUT\" IN EITHER COPY\n  ✗ level flag prints \"--10g-1eve1 4\"\n\nCISCO ASA  (pp76-83)\n  TWO FORMATS: DEFAULT and EMBLEM\n  show logging · show logging history · grep\n  Table 15.9 levels of logging\n  Table 15.10 = 10 message IDs, 9 descriptions\n  (106015 106016 106017 106018 106020 106021\n   106022 106023 106100 710003)\n\nCHECK POINT  (pp84-88)\n  fw log · $FwDIR · real-time flag \"-ftn\"\n  unification mode: INITIAL / SEMI / RAW\n  ✗ syntax printed TWICE (p85, p87) and NEITHER\n    is clean; the parameter tables can't be paired",
      "x": 1560,
      "y": -3420,
      "width": 620,
      "height": 700
    },
    {
      "id": "b7",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "LO06 · ROUTERS  (pp89-97)\n\nWHAT A CISCO ROUTER LOG MESSAGE IS\n  max 80 CHARACTERS, a leading PERCENT SIGN,\n  then an optional sequence number or timestamp\n  ✗ \"do not contain NUMERICAL IDENTIFIERS that\n    assist in identifying the messages\"\n\nSEVERITY 0-7 + THE UNIX NAMES  (Table 15.12)\n  0 Emergencies   LOG EMERG\n  1 Alerts       LOG ALERT\n  2 Critical     LOG CRIT\n  3 Errors       LOG ERR\n  4 Warnings     LOG WARNING\n  5 Notifications LOG NOTICE\n  6 Informational LOG INFO\n  7 Debugging    LOG DEBUG\n  RULE: \"The LOWER severity number represents a\n  HIGHER severity and vice-versa\"\n\nTable 15.11 — 10 %SEC- mnemonics\n  ✗ the page DUPLICATES IPACCESLOGP,\n    IPACCESLOGDP and IPACCESLOGNP as rows 8-10\n  ✗ prints \"level O to 7\" with the letter O\n\nCONFIGURATION\n  service timestamps log [datetime | log]\n  show logging · show logging history\n  show logging | include 172.16.1.92 .* \\(137\\)\n  ✗ p93/p94 figure unreadable beyond the\n    sequence numbers 002092-002097 — not reproduced",
      "x": -2400,
      "y": -2620,
      "width": 620,
      "height": 700,
      "color": "4"
    },
    {
      "id": "b8",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "LO07 · WEB SERVERS — IIS  (pp98-106)\n\nTHREE DEFAULT LOCATIONS, THREE CASE VARIANTS\n  IIS 6.0  %system32%\\LogFiles\\W3SVCN\n  IIS 7.0  %SystemDrive%\\Inetpub\\Logs\\\n           LogFiles\\w3svcN\n  IIS 8/10 %SystemDrive%\\inetpub\\logs\\LogFiles\n\nTable 15.15  W3C EXTENDED FIELDS  (pp101-103)\n  date · time · s-ip · cs-method\n  · cs-uri-stem · cs-uri-query · s-port\n  · cs-username · c-ip · cs(User-Agent)\n  · cs(Referer) · sc-status · sc-substatus\n  · sc-win32-status · time-taken\n\nTable 15.16  IIS vs NCSA\n  IIS  → fixed, COMMA-separated, local time\n  NCSA → fixed, SPACE-separated, local time\n         \"used for WEBSITES and not for FTP\n          sites\"\n  ✗ the page's own example prints the date two\n    ways: 03/6/11 in the line, 03/06/2011 in the\n    table\n\nTHE WALKTHROUGH  (pp105-106)\n  file  u_ex180405.log\n  path  C:\\inetpub\\logs\\LogFiles\\W3SVC1\\\n  #Software: Microsoft Internet Information\n    Services 10.0 · #version: 1.0\n\n  2018-04-05 12:43:58 195.129.104.112 GET\n  /ECWebsite - 80 - 213.69.168.60 Mozilla/5.0\n  (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64)\n  AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko)\n  Chrome/52.0.2743.116 Safari/537.36\n  Edge/15.15063 - 301 0 0 1076\n\n  THE PUNCHLINE: sc-status = \"the CAPABILITY of\n  the server in responding to requests\". The\n  capture shows the SAME request answered\n  301 and then 403, sc-substatus 14 on the 403.",
      "x": -1740,
      "y": -2620,
      "width": 620,
      "height": 700
    },
    {
      "id": "b9",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "LO07 · WEB SERVERS — APACHE  (pp108-115)\n\nTWO LOGS: access_log and error_log\n  $ tail -100 <access log>\n\n✗ p108 CONTRADICTS ITSELF: the default path is\n  given as /var/log/httpd/access_log, then the\n  RHEL command runs against\n  /etc/httpd/logs/access_log. Same split on p109.\n\nTable 15.17  LogFormat directives\n  %h client host · %l remote logname\n  %u authenticated user · %t time\n  %r request line · %>s status · %b bytes\n  %a client IP · %A server IP\n  ✗ p110 prints %l as the digit 1 in the table\n    while the same page's example prints %l\n\nTable 15.18  COMMON format\n  ✗ prints \"%{Referrer}\" with a DOUBLE R while\n    the example and the prose print \"Referer\"\n\nTable 15.19  COMBINED format  ← DEFECT\n  legible: Filename %f · Request Method %m\n    · Transport Protocol %H · Request Stem %U\n    · Time to Serve %T\n  ✗ \"Server Port\" AND \"Server Process ID\" BOTH\n    print  %P\n  ✗ 5 directives illegible → left BLANK:\n    Request Query String · Server Name · Session\n    Identifier Field · Visitor Identifier Field\n    · General Purpose Fields 1-10",
      "x": -1080,
      "y": -2620,
      "width": 620,
      "height": 700
    },
    {
      "id": "b10",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "LO08 · WHY + THE THREE TIERS  (pp116-120)\n\nWHY CENTRALIZE (p117-118)\n  ✗ \"reducing disk 1/0 on application servers\"\n  ✗ figure: \"metrics defined IN the log\"  vs\n    prose: \"defined ON the log\"\n  ✗ figure lists 4 CLM capabilities, prose 9\n\nTHREE TIERS — the only three in the module\n  1 LOG GENERATOR\n  2 LOG ANALYSIS AND STORAGE\n  3 LOG MONITORING\n  \"Clock daemon\" and \"Security/authorization\n  messages\" are FACILITY names, NOT tiers\n\n  ✗ p119 figure prints the box as \"Cdkction\n    Server\" and storage as \"MY SQL\" against p120's\n    \"collection servers\" / \"Oracle, MS SQL, etc.\"\n  storage examples: Oracle, MS SQL, etc.",
      "x": -420,
      "y": -2620,
      "width": 600,
      "height": 520
    },
    {
      "id": "b11",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "LO08 · THE SEVEN-STEP PIPELINE  (pp121-124)\n\n  1 Log Collection\n  2 Log Transmission\n  3 Log Storage\n  4 Normalization\n  5 Log Correlation\n  6 Log Analysis\n  7 Alerting and Reporting   ← ONE step\n\n  ✗ p152 MODULE SUMMARY ENUMERATES EIGHT —\n    it SPLITS step 7 into \"alerting\" and\n    \"reporting\". Not reconciled.\n  ✗ step 4 has THREE printed wordings:\n    \"Normalization\" (figure) / \"Step 4: Log\n    Normalization\" (p137) / \"log normalization\" (p152)\n  ✗ p152 drops \"logging\" from the p121 triad\n    (\"logging, monitoring, AND ANALYSIS of logs\")\n\nTRANSPORT — described in prose\n  syslog UDP · syslog TCP · ENCRYPTED SYSLOG\n  · HTTP/HTTPS · SOAP over HTTP\n  named with NO detail: SNMP, FTP/SCP\n\n  ✗ NO SYSLOG PORT NUMBER IS PRINTED ANYWHERE\n    in pp125-134, though p126 names \"the standard\n    syslog port\". Nothing supplied from outside.",
      "x": 240,
      "y": -2620,
      "width": 600,
      "height": 520,
      "color": "1"
    },
    {
      "id": "b12",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "LO08 · SYSLOG + STORAGE  (pp125-139)\n\nRFC 5424 FORMAT  (p125 / p129)\n  TIMESTAMP HOSTNAME TAG MESSAGEID\n           STRUCTURED-DATA MSG\n  ✗ p129 prints \"MESSAGED\" — quoted, not fixed\n  APP-NAME → originator; NIL if unavailable\n  PROCID   → process name or ID; NIL if none\n  MSGID    → the TYPE of message sent\n\nTable 15.20  FACILITIES  ← DEFECT\n  p130 prints codes 1-12, p131 prints 13, 14, 15,\n  16-23 — but THIRTEEN names print first\n  ✗ CODE 0 IS NEVER PRINTED → alignment off by\n    one, NOT repairable. Reproduced as printed.\n\nTable 15.21  SEVERITIES\n  Emerg Alert Crit Error Warn Notice Info Debug\n  ✗ NAMES ONLY — NO NUMERIC COLUMN AT ALL.\n    The 0-7 numbering is NOT asserted here.\n\nCOLLECTOR TOOLS  (p133-134)\n  Kiwi Syslog Server · Splunk Light · WhatsUp Gold\n  · Syslog-NG · SNMPSoft Sys-log Watcher\n  · NxLog · FastVue\n  ✗ p133 CONTRADICTS ITS OWN CAPTIONS TWICE:\n    figure kiwisyslog.com vs prose solarwinds.com;\n    figure netadmintools.com vs prose ezfive.com\n  ✗ figure prints \"sysbg-ng\", \"fastvue.c0\",\n    \"https.//www.whatsupgold.com\"\n\nSTORAGE + NORMALIZATION  (pp135-139)\n  3 decision headings (slide order ≠ prose order)\n  parsing regex figure; \"bad event\" figure\n  \"Common Event Expression (CEE)\" named ONCE,\n  no schema printed\n  ✗ pp137/138/139 print the SAME event stream\n    three times with three corrupted forms of\n    every address token",
      "x": 900,
      "y": -2620,
      "width": 620,
      "height": 700
    },
    {
      "id": "b13",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "LO08 · CORRELATION, ANALYSIS, CHALLENGES  (pp140-152)\n\nSTEP 5  LOG CORRELATION\n  \"matching a series of NORMALIZED log data to\n   determine a set of related events based on a\n   certain set of rules\"\n  why it is \"very critical and complicated\":\n    · some logs are human plain language, others\n      \"cryptic … with only esoteric system codes\"\n    · SILOED LENSES — a network IDS looks only at\n      packets and streams; an application only at\n      sessions, users, requests\n    · logs are STATIC — they hold no context of\n      ongoing events\n    · the same event on two systems of the same\n      version may not give the same information\n\nTWO TYPES\n  MICRO-LEVEL (=\"atomic\") — fields WITHIN a single\n    event; only when raw data is NORMALIZED;\n    → field correlation + rule correlation\n  MACRO-LEVEL — 6 inputs: rule · vulnerability\n    · profile (fingerprint) · anti-port\n    · watch list · geographic location\n    ✗ ONLY 3 ARE DESCRIBED (vulnerability, profile,\n      anti-port). Watch list and geographic location\n      are NAMED and NEVER defined.\n\nSTEP 6  MANUAL vs AUTOMATED\n  manual → \"experience and knowledge of the\n  NETWORK DEFENDER\" (slide: \"EXAMINER\")\n  automated → all phases sequentially with\n  minimal human interaction\n\n12 BEST PRACTICES  (p144 prose; slide prints 5)\n  NTP-synchronize the analysis system\n  PROACTIVE, not reactive\n  automate · review at regular intervals\n  develop a baseline · set a strategy to log\n  effective log format · collect CENTRALLY, SEPARATE\n  from production · end-to-end logging · correlate\n  data sources · unique identifiers · real-time\n\nCHALLENGES (p150-151)\n  prose: 4 sub-items under generation and storage\n  slide: 3 bullets, ✗ NEVER ALIGNED",
      "x": 1560,
      "y": -2620,
      "width": 620,
      "height": 700
    },
    {
      "id": "pat",
      "type": "text",
      "text": "THE MODULE'S FIVE TRAPS  (p152 summary aside,\nthis is what the exam tests)\n\n1 · THE 0-7 SCALE IS NAMED FIVE WAYS\n  p60  firewall   emerg/alert/crit/error/warning/\n                notification/informational/debugging\n  p43  Linux     Emergency, Alert, Critical, Error,\n                Waming, Notice, Info (+Debug in prose)\n  p77  ASA       Emergencies, Alerts, Critical, Errors,\n                Warnings, Notifications, Info, Debug\n  p91  router    same set + LOG EMERG…LOG DEBUG\n  p131 syslog    Emerg, Alert, Crit, Error, Warn,\n                Notice, Info, Debug\n  and the NUMBER is printed beside the NAME on\n  only some: p60 and p91 print \"O\" (letter) while\n  the prose prints \"0 to 7\"; p43 leaves the\n  Emergency value cell BLANK; p131 prints NO\n  numbers at all.\n\n2 · MISMATCHED TABLE COLUMNS\n  15.1 · 15.3 · 15.5 · 15.7 · 15.8 · 15.10 ·\n  15.11 · 15.19 · 15.20 each print a different\n  number of names and descriptions. Listed as two\n  UNPAIRED columns, never as a joined table.\n\n3 · THE PAGE CONTRADICTS ITSELF\n  p63 vs p65  Windows FW log location\n  p69 vs p71  Mac FW log location\n  p108        /var/log/httpd/ vs /etc/httpd/logs/\n  p85 vs p87  the fw log syntax\n  p118        IN the log vs ON the log\n  p125 vs p129 MESSAGEID vs MESSAGED\n  p133        every tool Source: caption\n  p143        automated vs automatic; EXAMINER\n              vs NETWORK DEFENDER\n\n4 · THE MODULE PRINTS DAMAGE AND MEANS IT\n  \"Ox\" for hex zero throughout · \"Waming\" ·\n  \"08a—ACCEEPT\" · \"endtirne\" · \"-8s\" for %s ·\n  \"'/vat/ log/btmp'\" · \"-alert\" for .alert ·\n  \"--10g-1eve1 4\" · \"MESSAGED\" · \"Tear down in\n  connection\" · \"%P\" twice. Reproduced VERBATIM.\n  Do not \"fix\" any of them.\n\n5 · WHAT IS NOT PRINTED ANYWHERE\n  the syslog port number · the expansion of\n  \"CLM\" (used once, p152) · the Mac user-specific\n  log folder · Table 15.21's numeric column · five\n  of Table 15.19's directives · any version number\n  · the CEE schema · 0 in Table 15.20",
      "x": -2400,
      "y": -1820,
      "width": 2040,
      "height": 700,
      "color": "6"
    }
  ],
